  "gameGuessEmptyBody": "To play you need a few memories. Add some richer memories and come back.",
  "guessInfoTitle": "Guess together",
  "guessInfoWhat": "The app asks you a specific question about one of your memories (a year, a place, a person, an event). You answer with voice or text, then you hear the right answer taken from the memory.",
  "guessInfoWhy": "Answering a specific question is active recall: memory consolidates better when you interrogate it rather than rereading passively. Spaced Retrieval Training, validated by RCTs in people with mild cognitive impairment, is exactly this — short question, answer, warm feedback. No scores, no underlined mistakes: if the answer isn't there, the app gently brings you back to the fact.",
  "guessInfoSource1Label": "USMART RCT (2017) — tablet-based spaced retrieval training in MCI",
  "guessInfoSource1Url": "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13195-017-0290-6",
  "guessInfoSource2Label": "Roediger & Karpicke (2006) — testing effect, remember by asking",
  "guessInfoSource2Url": "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.2006.01693.x",

  "gameStoryTitle": "Continue the story",
  "gameStoryShort": "You finish it",
  "gameStoryPrompt": "…and then what happened?",
  "gameStoryContinueHint": "You continue the memory… (speak or type)",
  "gameStorySendCta": "Let's see how it goes",
  "gameStoryAnotherCta": "Another memory",
  "gameStoryFullMemoryLabel": "The full memory",
  "gameStoryEmptyBody": "To play you need memories that are a bit long (at least two sentences). Add a richer memory and come back.",
  "storyInfoTitle": "Continue the story",
  "storyInfoWhat": "The app reads aloud the first sentences of one of your memories, then asks you to continue it. You tell how you think it went, and the app gives you warm feedback comparing with the original memory. At the end you see the full memory.",
  "storyInfoWhy": "Continuing a memory aloud from a narrative cue is the closest thing to how autobiographical memory naturally unfolds: not a closed quiz, but a story that unrolls. Guided narrative stimulation has shown benefits on episodic autobiographical memory in people with mild cognitive impairment, especially when the material is personal.",
  "storyInfoSource1Label": "Cotelli et al. (2012) — narrative cognitive rehabilitation in MCI/AD",
  "storyInfoSource1Url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22466023/",
  "storyInfoSource2Label": "Woods et al., Cochrane Review (2018) — reminiscence therapy in dementia",
  "storyInfoSource2Url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD001120.pub3",
  "recognizeNoPhotosBody": "To play you need memories with a photo. Add a photo to a memory first, then come back here.",

