  "recognizeInfoTitle": "Recognize and tell",
  "recognizeInfoWhat": "You see a photo from your album and you say out loud what you remember. There's no right or wrong answer: what matters is that your voice goes back to the memory. At the end the app gives you a small recap of what you said.",
  "recognizeInfoWhy": "Putting a memory back into words while looking at a familiar cue is the core of reminiscence therapy: a one-to-one conversation between a person and their own memory, mediated here by the app instead of a relative. Cochrane reviews show small but consistent benefits on mood, cognition and quality of life — especially when the cue is autobiographical, not generic.",
  "recognizeInfoSource1Label": "Woods et al., Cochrane Review (2018) — reminiscence therapy in dementia",
  "recognizeInfoSource1Url": "https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD001120.pub3",
  "recognizeInfoSource2Label": "Berry et al. (2014) — SenseCam and autobiographical memory",
  "recognizeInfoSource2Url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16194452/",

  "photoMatchInfoTitle": "Photo memory",
  "photoMatchInfoWhat": "A classic memory game: 6 face-down pairs of photos, you flip two cards at a time until you've matched them all. The photos come from your own album — not generic icons but faces, places and moments you already know.",
  "photoMatchInfoWhy": "Visual recognition memory is relatively preserved even in mild cognitive impairment, and serious games built on personalized autobiographical material are a stronger emotional engager than a generic memory game. No magic: it's about spending some active time inside your own photos.",
  "photoMatchInfoSource1Label": "JMIR Serious Games (2024) — meta-analysis of cognitive games in MCI/AD",
  "photoMatchInfoSource1Url": "https://games.jmir.org/2024/1/e55785",

  "homeTodayInfoTitle": "Today",
  "homeTodayInfoWhat": "Every day the app picks a memory you haven't walked in a while and shows it at the top of the home. If you open it and walk it again, its \"footprints\" brighten and the app waits longer before suggesting it again. If you leave it, it comes back sooner.",
  "homeTodayInfoWhy": "It's called Spaced Retrieval Training: refreshing a piece of information at intervals that grow over time (1, 3, 7, 14, 30 days) is one of the non-pharmacological techniques with the strongest evidence for memory in mild cognitive impairment and mild dementia. It works best when the material is personal and when the interval adapts to how recall is going.",
  "homeTodayInfoSource1Label": "Hopper et al. (2013) — review of spaced-retrieval interventions",
  "homeTodayInfoSource1Url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23886395/",
  "homeTodayInfoSource2Label": "USMART RCT (2017) — tablet-based SRT in MCI patients",
  "homeTodayInfoSource2Url": "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13195-017-0290-6"
